to get the opponent to score as many points as possible.

The problem with NetHack's score, and the reason why I believe it can't
be completely fixed, is that it's trying to measure two things, which
are incompatible.
Within a single game, the score as it is now is a reasonable indicator
of how far you've come. Higher score: closer to finishing this game.
Thus, for a single player who always plays in the same style, score is a
good measure of how well he's doing.
Between games, score depends on a lot more than progress. For two
_finished_ games, which finished in the same way, the one with the lower
score is usually the better one. Thus, for a single player whose style
is evolving, or for a comparison between players who both ascended,
score is a good measure of how _bad_ you're doing.

Put roughly and inaccurately, the current NetHack score is a good score
for games that are running or ended in death; it is a negative score for
games that have ascended similar characters; and it is no measure at all
for wildly dissimilar characters. And in the latter case, I don't think
a coherent scoring system can be devised at all. After all, how _do_ you
compare a speed ascension of a dwarven valkyrie with a tourist
extinctionist?

